1 | go-backend | Give the assistant channel its own token scope and the permission rows the epic says it needs, and nothing else. READ FIRST: issue 2799 in full; issue 2797 for the verified facts; the W0 spec page docs/prd/pages/assistant-channel.md on main or on branch docs/prd-assistant-channel-w0 if not yet merged; apps/api/internal/middleware/auth.go in full, especially ScopeConcierge at line 27, enforceScope around lines 117 to 132, and HasActiveBinding at lines 63 and 141; apps/api/internal/handler/routes.go around lines 412 to 425 for conciergeAuthOpts and lines 695 to 745 for the result-sessions split; apps/api/internal/middleware/auth_scope_test.go and the enforcement matrix in hermes_scope_enforcement_test.go; apps/api/internal/config/config.go lines 860 to 875 for CORS. THE WORK, four parts, all in apps/api. ONE, the scope. Add a second named scope constant for the assistant channel next to ScopeConcierge. Add WithScopeAllowed for it on exactly these route groups and no others: POST parse, the result-sessions writes that concierge already has, plus the result-sessions reads that concierge is denied at routes.go lines 735 and 741, plus the result-sessions PATCH at line 742 for update_trip. Do NOT allow it on the pay-link route; that is W3. Everything else stays default-deny 403. The middleware knows only one scope by name today and auth_scope_test.go line 154 rejects unknown ones, so this is route-group wiring plus tests, not a config flag. TWO, per-host liveness. HasActiveBinding takes only a userID, so revoking one channel binding does not kill a token if any other binding for that user is active, which couples this channel to Telegram in both directions. Add a channel-aware check so an assistant-scoped token is live only while a binding on the assistant channel value from W1-A is active, and a concierge token is unaffected by assistant bindings. Read the W1-A migration on main for the exact channel value. THREE, the service secret. The new service must not present HERMES_SERVICE_TOKEN; a leak of that is user impersonation. Add a second service-token env var and secret wiring following the pattern at routes.go around line 777 and docs/operations/env-vars-hermes.md, documented in docs/operations/env-vars-api.md. Do not create the Cloud Run secret; document the name and say in the PR body that an operator creates it. FOUR, CORS. config.go around line 868 defaults to two localhost origins. Make production origins configurable by env without changing the default, and document the var. TESTS ARE THE POINT. The enforcement matrix must gain rows for the new scope and keep every existing 403 assertion byte for byte. Add, and prove they FAIL on the parent commit in a throwaway worktree: an assistant-scoped token is 403 on bookings, saved cards, delete-account and the pay-link; an assistant-scoped token is 200 on result-session read and list; a concierge token is still 403 on those reads; revoking the assistant binding kills the assistant token and leaves a live concierge token untouched; and, per the fact at auth.go line 128, a token with an EMPTY scope is still never scope-gated, asserted explicitly so nobody later assumes otherwise. Paste real exit codes captured directly rather than read after a pipe. Run go build, go vet, the api unit suite with race, golangci-lint and make check-api-spec, and paste real exit codes. Commit before you break anything; never git stash; never git checkout to revert. Open a PR referencing issue 2799 with Refs and no closing keyword, since the authorization server and the tile are still to come. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name in commit or PR text, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Do not merge. | feat/ac-w1b-scope

2 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the wave 1 change on branch feat/ac-w1b-scope. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. The harness pushes the branch it checks out, so any commit on a producer ref is a production incident. Permitted writes are a slash tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: can an assistant-scoped token reach anything it should not, and can revoking it fail to take effect. Do not trust the diff; drive the real router. FIRST, enumerate every route group in routes.go and, for each, state what an assistant-scoped token gets: 200, 403 or 404. Compare that table to the allowlist in issue 2799 and the W0 spec. Any 200 outside parse, result-session create, refine, read, list and patch is a BLOCK. The pay-link must be 403. SECOND, confirm the existing enforcement matrix lost no assertion: diff the test file against the parent and confirm every prior 403 row survives unchanged. THIRD, liveness: mint an assistant token, revoke its binding, confirm the next request is refused; then confirm a concierge token for the same user was not affected, and the reverse. A liveness check that still keys on any binding for the user is a BLOCK, because it means disconnecting the assistant does not disconnect it. FOURTH, the empty-scope fact at auth.go line 128 must be asserted by a test, not inherited silently, because connect minting an unscoped token is how the assistant would reach everything. FIFTH, confirm the new service secret is a distinct env var and that nothing in the diff reads HERMES_SERVICE_TOKEN for the new service. SIXTH, confirm the CORS change does not alter the default and that the tests still pass with the default. SEVENTH, confirm the fail-first claims by checking out the parent in a throwaway worktree and running the new tests yourself. EIGHTH, no long dash, no vendor name, no trailer. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC ASSISTANT CHANNEL W1B SCOPE, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE and per-finding file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | feat/ac-w1b-scope
